WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.445
This is one that's kind of controversial.

2
00:00:02.445 --> 00:00:04.065
I posted this on LinkedIn a while

3
00:00:04.065 --> 00:00:05.465
back and it generated quite a bit

4
00:00:05.465 --> 00:00:06.625
of buzz.

5
00:00:06.625 --> 00:00:09.405
Not every account deserves equal time.

6
00:00:09.545 --> 00:00:11.105
Now I know that might rub some

7
00:00:11.105 --> 00:00:12.785
people the wrong way,

8
00:00:12.785 --> 00:00:14.865
particularly if you've got a sales manager

9
00:00:14.865 --> 00:00:16.005
or you've been in the business a

10
00:00:16.005 --> 00:00:17.965
long time because we've all heard it.

11
00:00:17.965 --> 00:00:19.805
Hey I haven't seen your salesperson here

12
00:00:19.805 --> 00:00:21.145
in three months.

13
00:00:21.145 --> 00:00:22.585
What's first thing that happens?

14
00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:24.005
Sales manager picks up the phone and

15
00:00:24.005 --> 00:00:25.705
asks, hey, why haven't you been calling

16
00:00:25.705 --> 00:00:26.905
on this customer?

17
00:00:26.905 --> 00:00:28.045
I get it.

18
00:00:28.045 --> 00:00:29.705
I've been on both sides of that

19
00:00:29.705 --> 00:00:31.665
conversation, but here's the reality.

20
00:00:31.665 --> 00:00:32.985
We only have so many hours in

21
00:00:32.985 --> 00:00:34.805
the day, so many days in the

22
00:00:34.805 --> 00:00:36.485
week, and so many opportunities to get

23
00:00:36.485 --> 00:00:37.825
in front of customers.

24
00:00:37.825 --> 00:00:39.505
If we're trying to call every account

25
00:00:39.505 --> 00:00:41.645
in our territory with the same frequency,

26
00:00:41.705 --> 00:00:43.365
we're probably not spending enough time with

27
00:00:43.365 --> 00:00:45.785
the accounts that represent our greatest opportunity.

28
00:00:45.865 --> 00:00:46.785
And that's where I think we need

29
00:00:46.785 --> 00:00:47.965
to be more intentional.
